--TEST--
`Core\Uri`'s four percent-coders are two inverse pairs: each decoder undoes its own encoder over every ASCII byte, the two encoders disagree on exactly the two the form encoding spells differently, and above `U+007F` they are one encoding — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 2.1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12 has two encodings because the wire has two, and the pairs are named for
// where the answer goes rather than for which C function they came from. The
// neighbouring cases assert what each member does to a handful of bytes; this
// one asks the two questions that only a sweep can answer — is each pair
// actually inverse, and is the disagreement between them *exactly* the two
// bytes the module's own table names, rather than those two plus one nobody
// wrote a row for.
int $componentPair = 0;
int $formPair = 0;
int $formReadsComponent = 0;
int $componentReadsForm = 0;
int $sameSpelling = 0;
string $differ = "";
for (int $b = 0; $b < 128; $b = $b + 1) {
    var $c = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($b as uint);
    var $ec = Core\Uri::encodeComponent($c);
    var $ef = Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($c);
    // Each decoder undoes its own encoder. Nothing here is exempt: a NUL, a
    // control byte, every reserved delimiter and every unreserved character
    // all go out and come back as themselves.
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeComponent($ec) as string) == $c) { $componentPair = $componentPair + 1; }
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($ef) as string) == $c) { $formPair = $formPair + 1; }
    // The two cross directions are not symmetric, and that asymmetry is the
    // reason the member names have to carry the difference: the form decoder
    // reads `%20` as well as `+`, so it undoes the component encoder over the
    // whole sweep, while the component decoder reads only `%20` and therefore
    // misses on exactly one byte.
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($ec) as string) == $c) { $formReadsComponent = $formReadsComponent + 1; }
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeComponent($ef) as string) == $c) { $componentReadsForm = $componentReadsForm + 1; }
    if ($ec == $ef) {
        $sameSpelling = $sameSpelling + 1;
    } else {
        // Not counted — spelled out, so a third disagreement could not hide
        // inside a number that still looked plausible.
        $differ = $differ . " " . $ec . "/" . $ef;
    }
}
echo "component pair ", $componentPair, "/128\n";
echo "form pair ", $formPair, "/128\n";
echo "form reads component ", $formReadsComponent, "/128\n";
echo "component reads form ", $componentReadsForm, "/128\n";
echo "same spelling ", $sameSpelling, "/128 ->", $differ, "\n";

// Above `U+007F` the two are one encoding, because percent-encoding is defined
// over octets and no octet of a multi-byte sequence is in either unreserved
// set. The escape count is the UTF-8 length of the sweep — 2 octets for the
// first five code points, 3 for the next two, 4 for the last three — which is
// what "one escape per octet, not per character" means when it is counted
// rather than shown on one row.
array<uint> $high = [128, 160, 233, 255, 256, 8364, 65533, 65536, 128512, 1114111];
int $agree = 0;
int $trips = 0;
int $escapes = 0;
foreach ($high as uint $cp) {
    var $h = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($cp);
    var $hc = Core\Uri::encodeComponent($h);
    if ($hc == Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($h)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeComponent($hc) as string) == $h) { $trips = $trips + 1; }
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($hc) as string) == $h) { $trips = $trips + 1; }
    $escapes = $escapes + (Core\Str::countOf($hc, "%") as int);
}
echo "high agree ", $agree, "/10, trips ", $trips, "/20, escapes ", $escapes, "\n";
echo Core\Uri::encodeComponent(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(128512)), "\n";

// The one byte the sweep above found, named on its own line so the count has a
// witness: a form-encoded space read as a URI component is a literal `+`, not
// a space. This is the bug the pair split exists to make impossible to write
// by accident, and the type system cannot catch it — both members take a
// `string` and answer one.
echo "[", (Core\Uri::decodeComponent(Core\Uri::encodeFormValue(" ")) as string), "]\n";

// The sweep stops at `U+007F` because past it a *lone* octet is not text: the
// decoders answer `string`, so `%FF` throws rather than answering (the
// module's gap 2 — the honest signature is `: bytes`, and the throw is what
// `rule:types/conversion`'s checked `bytes as string` row would do one line later). An
// octet sequence that *is* UTF-8 decodes, which is the other side of the same
// bound.
try {
    echo (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%FF") as string), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $lone) {
    echo "lone octet: refused\n";
}
echo (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%C3%A9") as string), "\n";
--EXPECT--
component pair 128/128
form pair 128/128
form reads component 128/128
component reads form 127/128
same spelling 126/128 -> %20/+ ~/%7E
high agree 10/10, trips 20/20, escapes 28
%F0%9F%98%80
[+]
lone octet: refused
é
